  "question": {
    "text": "An ideal gas undergoes a reversible isothermal expansion from state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> to state II followed by a reversible adiabatic expansion from state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math>&nbsp;to state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>III</mi><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;The correct plot&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mfenced></math> representing the changes from state&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> to state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>III</mi></math>&nbsp;is(are)<p>(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math>: pressure, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>: volume, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math>: temperature, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math>: enthalpy, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></math>: entropy)</p>",
    "image": null
  },
